CORE-001 Protocol — Licensing
================================

This repository is licensed under a single license, with one narrow
carve-out. It used to be split in two, with the specification text under
CC BY 4.0 and everything else under Apache-2.0. That split is now removed.

1. SPECIFICATION TEXT, REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATION, TEST VECTORS, AND TOOLS
   Paths: spec/**, tools/**, vectors/**, and every other file in this
   repository except the carve-out in section 2.
   License: Apache License, Version 2.0
   Full text: https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
   You may use, modify, and distribute these files, including for
   commercial purposes, under the terms of that license.

   Why a single license, and why this one
   --------------------------------------
   An implementer needs the specification text and an implementation of
   it to carry the same permissions. Apache-2.0 section 3 grants an
   express patent license. The Creative Commons licenses state that they
   do not grant patent rights at all, and Creative Commons itself advises
   against using them for software. A specification that implementers are
   expected to build on, but which carries no patent grant, exposes those
   implementers to patent claims on the very material they were invited
   to implement. That is the wrong risk to hand to the people this
   protocol is asking to build on it, so the specification text is under
   the same license as the code.

2. CARVE-OUT
   ---------
   The following are not part of this license grant and must not be
   published or redistributed: signing private keys, release pipeline
   scripts and ledgers, customer lists, pricing rules, and internal
   decision records.

Trademarks
----------
Apache-2.0 section 6 does not grant trademark rights. Use of the protocol
name is governed separately, see the name usage policy.

NO CLAIM OF COMPLIANCE
----------------------
Provision of this material does not state or imply that any party is
compliant with any law, regulation, standard, or certification scheme.
It evidences only that an implementation is consistent with the
specification as published.

Filename convention
-------------------
Specification version:  MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH  (e.g. 1.0.0)
Package release date:   YYYY.MM             (e.g. 2026.10)
